⚠ WHAT THAT DOES NOT COVER, and it is the reason this notice did not simply
disappear at Rev 3: the power-loss test interrupts the struct lfs_config
CONTRACT at prog_size granularity.  NeurOne's medium is an eMMC behind an XTS
layer, which may tear inside a 512 B sector and whose erase semantics are not
the raw-flash semantics littlefs is written against (upstream #1083 asks exactly
this and has no answer).  No claim of power-loss atomicity may be made against
the MEDIUM from this directory or from that test — OI-LFS-07, and it needs
hardware.  Two further caller obligations came out of §11 and are not yet met:
OI-LFS-08 (upstream #1210's silent file loss, worst case ukmd.rec) and
OI-LFS-09 (validate stored values by content on every read).  See "What this
vendoring does not establish" below.

Why v2.11.3 and not an older, longer-in-the-field tag.  Stated because "newest"
is the entry a reviewer is most likely to second-guess.  The §11 evaluation
found no reason to move the pin.
  - v2.11.3 contains commit 488e84bb, "Fixed data corruption with multiple write
    handles": a sync to one handle left a second open handle on the same file
    stale, after which the allocator could re-use still-referenced blocks.  That
    is a DATA-CORRUPTION defect on exactly the axis L-1…L-4 rest on, and it is
    not present in any earlier v2 release.  Choosing an older tag would mean
    knowingly pinning a version carrying it.
  - The defect also has a NeurOne-side consequence that outlives the version
    choice, and it belongs with the OI-LOG-05..07 glue rather than here: no file
    on any NeurOne instance may be open through two handles at once.  The three
    Config files (the "NPMP" blob, Map 3's journal, ukmd.rec) and the two log
    files each have exactly one writer by design — that is now a requirement the
    glue has to keep, not an accident of how it happens to be written.
  - v2.11.3 is 11 commits ahead of v2.11.2; the remainder are a null-callback
    guard in lfs_dir_fetchmatch, implicit-conversion warning fixes, and README
    typos.  It is a patch release on the same on-disk version (2.1), so the pin
    carries no format migration.

